    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm looking into some issues with a recent asphalt overlay we did here in Phoenix. We're seeing some premature longitudinal cracking in a specific section. Can you give me a technical summary of the primary causes for this kind of pavement failure, specifically looking at how thermal expansion and bitumen viscosity play into it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. Longitudinal cracking in asphalt overlays, particularly in areas like Phoenix, Arizona, can be attributed to several factors. Thermal expansion plays a significant role, as the asphalt base expands in the heat and contracts during cooler nighttime temperatures, leading to tensile stresses. Bitumen viscosity also affects pavement performance. If the bitumen has been specified with an inappropriate viscosity, it may not effectively bond with the aggregate in warmer conditions, contributing to the formation of longitudinal cracks as the mix loses cohesion under stress. It’s critical to match bitumen viscosity specifications to the local climate.",
      "source": "target",
